.TH CRON\-TRANSLATE 1 "2026-09" "cron-translate" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
cron-translate \- cron expressions in human terms
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B cron-translate
.I EXPRESSION
.RI [ options ]
.br
.B cron-translate convert
.B \-\-from
.I DIALECT
.B \-\-to
.I DIALECT
.I EXPRESSION
.RI [ options ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Explains a five-field cron expression in words, lists its next runs in a
chosen timezone, and flags the runs a daylight-saving transition will skip or
repeat. The wording of the description comes from cron-descriptor; the
next-run times come from croniter.
.PP
An
.I EXPRESSION
may also be an English phrase
.RB ( "every weekday at 9am" ).
Where cron cannot express the phrase \(em
.BR "every 2 weeks" ,
.BR "last Friday of the month" ,
.B every 90 minutes
\(em it says why and offers the closest expression rather than guessing.
.PP
.B convert
moves a schedule between cron dialects. See
.B DIALECTS
below.
.PP
Quote the expression: most of them contain characters the shell would eat.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-tz " ZONE"
IANA timezone for the next runs (default UTC).
.TP
.BI \-\-next " N"
How many next runs to show (default 3).
.TP
.B \-\-no\-dst\-check
Skip the daylight-saving checks: no per-run flags and no transition warnings.
.TP
.BI \-\-between " START END"
List every run in the closed interval, ISO 8601, e.g.
.BR 2026-07-15T00:00 .
.TP
.B \-\-json
Machine-readable output.
.SH CONVERT OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-from " DIALECT"
The dialect the expression is written in.
.TP
.BI \-\-to " DIALECT"
The dialect to write it in.
.PP
.BR \-\-tz ,
.BR \-\-next ,
.B \-\-no\-dst\-check
and
.B \-\-json
work as above. The next runs of both expressions are printed on the same
clock, the converted text having been parsed back with the target dialect's
own parser first.
.SH DIALECTS
.TP
.B vixie
Five-field crontab. Aliases:
.BR cron ", " crontab ", " unix .
.TP
.B k8s
Kubernetes CronJob
.BR spec.schedule .
Aliases:
.BR kubernetes ", " cronjob .
.TP
.B eventbridge
AWS EventBridge: six fields, a year, day-of-week 1-7 with 1 = Sunday. Alias:
.BR aws .
.TP
.B quartz
Six or seven fields, leading seconds,
.BR L ", " W " and " # .
.TP
.B systemd
.BR OnCalendar= .
Aliases:
.BR oncalendar ", " timer .
.PP
A conversion the target dialect cannot express fails rather than print a
wrong answer; one that is exact but reads differently prints a caveat.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The expression was understood.
.TP
.B 64
It could not be read as an expression or a phrase.
.TP
.B 65
It was read, and the target dialect cannot express it.
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
Explain a business-hours schedule:
.PP
.RS
.B cron-translate '*/15 9-17 * * 1-5'
.RE
.PP
Next five runs in Rome, with DST warnings:
.PP
.RS
.B cron-translate '0 3 * * *' \-\-tz Europe/Rome \-\-next 5
.RE
.PP
Move a Quartz trigger to EventBridge:
.PP
.RS
.B cron-translate convert \-\-from quartz \-\-to eventbridge '0 0 12 ? * MON-FRI *'
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR crontab (5),
.BR systemd.time (7).
Project documentation:
https://github.com/fabiocicerchia/cron-translate
